I just acquired a copy of Nethack this weekend and with great expectation
I booted it up and my first thought was, "THIS is what everybody's so
excited about?"

I admit I was biased since I'm an avid gamer and I've played the super-
sophisticated Ultimas, Might & Magics, et al.  What I've consistently
found with those games, however, is that they look pretty but their plots
are so intricate that if you screw up just once you're tempted to start
over.  With Nethack, when you screw up it's just part of the necessary
learning process.  I find this a refreshing change.

Also, yesterday I realized that not only do the dungeons change every
time you start over, but so do the scrolls, wands, rings, etc.  My first
response to that was, "THIS SUCKS!", because naturally I was compiling
a list of which potions did what and so on.  Later, I was able to accept
these changes as what made the game, and therefore each individual
character, unique.  There is no standard adventure formula I could easily
follow to conquer this game: everyone starts off equally confused.

I also grew to appreciate the strange ASCII symbolic representation of
everything.  These symbols allow you to do a lot of imagination which
makes the game more surreal; as opposed to games like Ultima, where you
are obviously in a two-dimensional world of the artist's imagination.

So, as you may have guessed, I am steadily becoming an avid Nethacker and
I'm opposed to "improvements" to this game to make it "more like Ultima."
Admittedly, I've only been playing Nethack for a few days, so I may change
my opinion over time.  But for right now, the only change I can suggest
is to allow freer passage in the hallways so I can run away from monsters
without my pet blocking the way (I have died many times due to this).

Overall, I find Nethack consistently challenging and I'm sure I'll be
spending many an hour playing it (reminds me of my youth dedicated to
exploring the Telengard Dungeon).

Anyway, keep the clues coming and above all: keep on hacking!

